# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the values you need. `.env` is git-ignored.
#
# This file holds secrets and deployment pointers only. Behaviour lives in
# `register/knobs.py`; no knob is settable from a config file, only from a
# GOVERNED_BI_* variable. The one exception is the access policy: set
# GOVERNED_BI_ACCESS_POLICY below and `govern/access.py::StaticRoleAccessPolicy.from_toml`
# reads a TOML you supply. (`src/` also ships two TOMLs of its own —
# `govern/adversarial.toml` and `register/arms.toml` — but those are declared
# claims, not operator configuration.) A real shell variable always beats `.env`.
#
# Full reference: docs/usage.md#environment

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The database. Serve and the eval driver both read GOVERNED_BI_PG_DSN first,
# then PG_RENAME_DECOY_DSN — that precedence is `credentials.PG_DSN_NAMES` and is
# declared in exactly one place.
GOVERNED_BI_PG_DSN=host=127.0.0.1 port=5432 dbname=... user=... password=...

# The second name is a real fallback, not an alias for the first: it is where the
# BIRD arm's warehouse lives (the renamed-and-decoyed Postgres from
# ../BIRD-Data-Obfuscation, `docker compose up pg_rename_decoy`). Set it only if
# you are serving the BIRD corpus, and set it *together with* GOVERNED_BI_CORPUS_DIR
# below -- the DSN and the corpus are **one switch**. A corpus's assets name tables
# that exist in one warehouse and not the other, so serving either over the other's
# database finds nothing, and the failure reads like a retrieval bug.
#
# With both names set the first one wins, silently. That is the intended way to
# park a DSN, but it means an edit to this line changes nothing until the line
# above is removed.
# PG_RENAME_DECOY_DSN=host=127.0.0.1 port=5435 dbname=bird user=bird password=...

# Model access on the `openai` gateway, which is the default for all three
# surfaces. Bedrock takes whatever boto3 resolves and so has no key variable;
# the proxy gateway reads its key from Secrets Manager.
OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...

# The semantic layer. Resolved against this repository's root, not the
# process's working directory.
GOVERNED_BI_CORPUS_DIR=../BIRD-corpus

# The other way to have a corpus: seed one from a live schema instead of curating
# it. `api/graph_app.py::make_graph` needs GOVERNED_BI_CORPUS_DIR *or* a corpus
# dropped into `corpora/` *or* this — with none of the three it refuses to start
# ("nothing to serve"). GOVERNED_BI_CORPUS_DIR wins if both are set, in which
# case this narrows the served corpus to that one schema instead of seeding.
#
# This is the variable the demo path in docs/usage.md tells you to set: run
# `tools/load_demo_schema.py`, leave GOVERNED_BI_CORPUS_DIR unset, and set this
# to gbi_demo_sales. Seeding from the live schema is how the corpus and the
# warehouse are kept from disagreeing. Changing it requires a restart.
# GOVERNED_BI_SCHEMA=gbi_demo_sales

# Where that seeded corpus is written. Unset means
# `runs/seeded-corpus/$GOVERNED_BI_SCHEMA`. Read only on the seeding path, so it
# does nothing when GOVERNED_BI_CORPUS_DIR is set.
# GOVERNED_BI_SEED_DIR=

# There is no transport credential. Removed 2026-08-13: no served route asks
# for one, so anything that can reach the port can drive the engine and read
# `/audit/turns`. Bind the server to loopback and treat the port as the
# boundary — docs/usage.md#serve-langgraph-server says what that re-exposes.

# A `StaticRoleAccessPolicy` TOML file (ADR 0012 §2). Unset means `OpenAccessPolicy`,
# which authorizes every table and is what this repository ships — so every
# authorization rule fires and every one of them says yes.
#
# Read by `api/graph_app.py::access_policy_from_environment` and resolved against the
# repository root, not the process's cwd, for the same reason GOVERNED_BI_CORPUS_DIR is:
# a relative path read from the wrong directory fails as "no such file" instead of
# "you are somewhere else". A path that does not exist is a startup RuntimeError, not a
# silent fallback to open.
#
# There is one principal here and nothing on the transport distinguishes callers, so a
# non-open policy narrows every caller identically. Per-caller grants need a second
# principal first — `docs/enterprise-fork.md` carries what that owes.
# GOVERNED_BI_ACCESS_POLICY=access-policy.toml

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Models
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Three surfaces take a model and each resolves its own provider: the agent that
# writes SQL, the utility model behind the scope gate and the facet rewriters,
# and the embedder. The ids do not fall back to one another — a cheap rewriter
# beside an expensive agent is the normal configuration.
#
# An embedding id is not portable across gateways: text-embedding-3-large on
# OpenAI, amazon.titan-embed-text-v2:0 on Bedrock.

# GOVERNED_BI_MODEL=
# GOVERNED_BI_MODEL_EFFORT=high
# GOVERNED_BI_UTILITY_MODEL=
# GOVERNED_BI_UTILITY_MODEL_EFFORT=
# GOVERNED_BI_EMBEDDING_MODEL=text-embedding-3-large
#
#   ^ SET THIS ONE. It reads as optional beside the overrides above and is not:
#     unset, the semantic retrieval channel reports `not_configured` on every
#     facet and routing runs on lexical matching alone. The failure is silent and
#     does not look like a configuration problem -- whether a question routes at
#     all then depends on whether the model happens to emit a query string that
#     literally matches a column name, so the *same* question answers once and
#     refuses the next time, and what you see is a wall of `no_schema_matched`
#     refusals that reads like a serving bug. Cost half a day on 2026-08-18.
#
#     Confirm it in one turn rather than trusting this comment: ask anything and
#     read `record.facet_hits[*].channels.semantic` -- "ran" is configured,
#     "not_configured" is not. Any measurement taken while it says the latter
#     measured a degraded system.

# Unset means `openai`. Set one variable for all three surfaces, or override a
# surface individually. Each value is a distinct arm and is recorded as
# llm_provider / llm_utility_provider / embedding_provider, because the same
# model id behind two gateways is two treatments that would otherwise share one
# config hash.
# GOVERNED_BI_PROVIDER=openai
# GOVERNED_BI_MODEL_PROVIDER=
# GOVERNED_BI_UTILITY_PROVIDER=
# GOVERNED_BI_EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=

# Bedrock only. Needs `uv sync --extra bedrock`. Credentials are whatever boto3
# resolves — static keys, AWS_PROFILE, or an instance or task role with nothing
# set here — so there is no key variable to list. Region is read in this order,
# the engine's own name first so it wins over whatever the shell exports for
# other tooling: GOVERNED_BI_AWS_REGION, AWS_REGION, AWS_DEFAULT_REGION.
# GOVERNED_BI_AWS_REGION=us-east-1

# Proxy gateway only. The first value is the *name* of a Secrets Manager secret
# holding the key and base URL, never the key itself.
# GOVERNED_BI_PROXY_SECRET=
# GOVERNED_BI_PROXY_REGION=
# GOVERNED_BI_PROXY_CA_BUNDLE=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Timeouts and retries
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Each of these is a comparability knob. Keep them identical across arms you
# intend to compare, or the arms differ by more than the thing under test.
# GOVERNED_BI_LLM_MAX_RETRIES=3
# GOVERNED_BI_LLM_TIMEOUT_S=300
# GOVERNED_BI_UTILITY_TIMEOUT_S=30

# These three are read env-first, ahead of their register knobs — and the record
# now follows. `register/knobs.py::env_override` reads the same variable with the
# same parsing rules the readers use, and `serve/session.py::_resolved_knobs`
# resolves the environment last "because that is the order the readers use", so
# `knobs_resolved` publishes what the run actually ran at. They used to outrank
# the record silently, which is what that function exists to have fixed. Blank is
# unset; a value the reader would choke on raises at session construction.
# GOVERNED_BI_AGENT_NODE_TIMEOUT_S=1200
# GOVERNED_BI_AGENT_RECURSION_LIMIT=40
# GOVERNED_BI_RAIL_NODE_TIMEOUT_S=120

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local state on disk
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# All three default under `runs/` and all three work unset. Set one only to move
# state off the default path -- a test that must not write into the real store, or
# a cache on a bigger disk.

# Checkpoint databases: served conversations, and the one-turn CLI plus the eval
# driver. Two files on purpose, so a 1,351-question benchmark run does not become
# the conversation history.
#
# Both are **file paths, not DSNs**, and `serve/checkpointer.py::assert_not_a_warehouse`
# refuses a value containing `host=`, `dbname=`, `password=`, `postgres://`,
# `postgresql://` or `redshift://` at configuration time rather than at the first
# turn. A checkpointer pointed at the analytics warehouse writes conversation
# state into it, and that is not recoverable by unsetting the variable afterwards.
# GOVERNED_BI_CONVERSATION_DB=runs/conversations.sqlite
# GOVERNED_BI_HARNESS_DB=runs/harness-checkpoints.sqlite

# Persistent embedding cache: one LanceDB database per model, one table per vector
# width, shared by the server, the CLI and the eval driver. Keeping it warm is why
# a retrieval-only arm costs nothing to re-run; deleting it re-pays every embedding
# call. The per-model subdirectory name is sanitised, because a Bedrock model id is
# not a legal directory name on Windows.
# GOVERNED_BI_VECTOR_CACHE=runs/vectors

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Do not set OPENAI_BASE_URL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# It redirects the chat client and the embedder together, because both build an
# OpenAI-shaped client and both read it. A gateway that serves a chat model but
# not an embedding model cannot be expressed this way. Use the per-surface
# provider variables above instead.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tracing — LangSmith, and only LangSmith
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Off by default. LangSmith bills one trace per root invocation, so a whole
# agentic turn costs one trace and a 20-question debug run costs 20. LangChain
# reads these variables itself; there is no callback to wire.
#
# When tracing is on, traces carry run inputs and outputs in full — tool
# messages, row previews, governed context. That is intended here: this
# repository is not production, and sensitive columns are filtered at the
# datasource before they can reach a tool message. A production deployment
# would need a masking layer at the tracer seam. There is none here.
# LANGSMITH_TRACING=true
# LANGSMITH_API_KEY=lsv2_...
# LANGSMITH_PROJECT=governed-bi
